  "material": {
    "verdict": "PASS recognisable physical material",
    "interpretation": "Weathered rough-dressed dark grey-brown mineral sandstone masonry with subdued warm patina; exact historic lithology is uncertain.",
    "observations": "Retained 1254 raw, canonical 512 RGB and actual Mid 256/High 512 visibly show coherent stone bedding/fractures, mineral aggregate, pits, worn/chipped arrises and recessed weathered joints. Granular detail is organized with the stone surface and larger fractures. It does not read as enlarged native pixels, flat abstraction or an applied uniform noise overlay. The raw and both tiers remain sharply photographic at their literal sizes."
  },
  "formsColourAndScale": {
    "verdict": "PASS representative native forms, with visible phase/proportion variation",
    "observations": "The original asymmetric bond is retained: narrow cropped top course, left column split above the main right-side course, narrow upper-middle block, broad upper-right block, broad lower-middle and cropped lower-right block. Warm grey-brown colour, low values and coarse features remain representative. Natural arrises replace the native pixel steps. The original cropped course remains a cropped course rather than an invented trim, small edge brick or frame.",
    "driftAssessment": "Several secondary joints shift visibly by a few native pixels and block proportions vary modestly. Bounded cross-sections independently measure main vertical 17->15, upper divider 33->30, lower divider 50->47 and right course 33->30. These are not exact recovered joint phases. At both 32 square and 16 square comparisons the asymmetric bond and relative coarse forms remain clearly similar. Literal repeats retain the same joint topology without an extra little brick, detached contact or new image-border seam. The numerical drift disclosure was evaluated alongside actual shape/edge views; it was not accepted solely because a self-review mentioned it.",
